\title{The algebraic branch: interpolation to rank sums}
\taxon{Overview}
\meta{dependencies}{None.}
\meta{provenance}{Expository organization and source review, 9 October 2026.}

\p{Let #{W} be a finite-dimensional real vector space. We use [univariate polynomial maps](cn-0064) #{P:\mathbb R\to W}: after choosing coordinates in #{W}, each coordinate of #{P(t)} is a scalar polynomial in #{t}. Choose distinct real [interpolation nodes](cn-0063) #{t_i} and require the value #{P(t_i)} to lie in a prescribed subspace #{U_i\subseteq W}. The route is evaluation dimensions, rank loss at those points, multiplicity of polynomial determinants, and a degree count. Graphs of linear maps then turn intersection dimensions into matrix ranks.}

